## Why I did it differently from the literal instruction

The task said "just call `AccountRatingService.recalculate(opp.AccountId)` for each opportunity." That approach issues **one `@future` call per opportunity**, which hits the 50-calls-per-transaction limit on the very first batch of 200. Even deduplicating by account would produce up to 200 future calls in the worst case.

Instead, the trigger collects the **distinct set of `AccountId` values** and makes a **single** `@future` call passing that set. Inside the future method, all SOQL and DML are bulk (one query for all opportunities, one for all accounts, one update for changed ratings). This keeps opportunity saves fast (one async dispatch), stays well within governor limits, and handles any batch size.

