## Explanation of changes

**Fan-out dispatcher instead of a one-by-one chain.** My previous solution chained each job to the next (job 1 enqueues job 2, job 2 enqueues job 3, …), which for 200 cases produces a chain 200 deep. Salesforce limits the async stack depth to **50**, so that approach throws `System.AsyncException: Maximum stack depth has been reached`.

The fix uses a **fan-out** pattern: a dispatcher job enqueues up to 49 individual `CaseSurveyJob` instances in its own transaction, plus one more dispatcher for the remaining IDs. Each dispatcher is a sibling in the async tree, not a deeper level for every case. For 200 cases the depth is only about 6 (5 dispatchers + 1 leaf), far under the 50 limit. Every case still gets its own isolated `CaseSurveyJob`, so a single failure cannot affect the others.

**Two constructors on `CaseSurveyJob`.**
- `CaseSurveyJob(Id)` – the normal path; processes a single case and creates the Task.
- `CaseSurveyJob(List<Id>)` – the dispatcher path; fans out individual jobs in chunks of 49 (reserving the 50th enqueue slot for the continuation dispatcher).

**Trigger logic unchanged in intent.** If ≤ 50 cases qualify, the trigger enqueues one job per case directly (the original requirement). If > 50, it enqueues a single dispatcher which handles the fan-out across multiple transactions.

## What went wrong

| Error | Fix |
|---|---|
| `new Map<Id,String>([SELECT Id, Name FROM User …])` | Apex can't initialise a `Map` from a `List<SObject>` directly; must build it in a loop. |
| `end` | Reserved keyword in Apex; renamed to `batchEnd`. |
| `emails.subList(i, end)` | `subList` doesn't exist on Apex `List`; not needed at all with the corrected approach. |
| `Messaging.EmailSendState` | Not a valid type in this environment. `Messaging.sendEmail(List<…>)` returns `void`; individual email failures are handled internally (the bad email is simply dropped, the rest go out). So we count the emails we successfully submitted. |
| **Root cause of the test failure** | 200 separate `sendEmail` calls → 200 email invocations > limit of 10. **Fix:** send all emails in **one** `Messaging.sendEmail` call (1 invocation, well within the limit). Salesforce already isolates per-email failures inside a single call. |

